---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { allTerms, groupBySection } from '../lib/terms';
import fs from 'node:fs';

const terms = await allTerms();
const groups = groupBySection(terms);
let links = 0;
try { links = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync('src/generated/graph.json', 'utf8')).edges.length; } catch {}
---
<Base
  title="About"
  description="What Jargonautica is, who writes it, how it is built, and the licence that lets you take it and use it."
>
  <h1>About</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Jargonautica is a glossary for people who got handed a word and no explanation. Every entry is
    written twice: once so anyone can follow it, and once so somebody who works in the field is not
    short-changed.
  </p>

  <h2>Why Twice</h2>
  <p>
    Most glossaries pick a side. Either they are so simplified that they are useless the moment you need
    to do anything, or they assume you already know the five terms sitting inside the definition. Writing
    every entry twice sidesteps that. The plain version comes first and never leans on jargon it has not
    explained. The technical version sits underneath a control you can open when you want it, and it is
    allowed to be properly technical: the maths, the trade-offs, the arguments practitioners are still
    having.
  </p>
  <p>
    The other rule is that nothing stands alone. Where an entry depends on another term it links to it,
    and each entry ends with a handful of related terms and a line explaining why each one is related.
    There are currently {terms.length} terms and {links} links between them, which is why there is{' '}
    <a href="/ai/graph/">a graph</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Who Writes It</h2>
  <p>
    Jargonautica is written and maintained by Big Trouble Labs ('BTL'). It started as one person's notes for keeping track of
    a field that invents vocabulary faster than it explains it, and grew until it seemed worth putting
    somewhere public. AI is the first glossary. The format is not specific to AI, so it will not be the
    last.
  </p>
  <p>
    Anyone whose suggestion turns into an entry is credited on that entry, by whatever name they ask for.
    <a href="/suggest/">Suggestions are open</a>, and no email address is ever collected.
  </p>

  <h2>The Licence</h2>
  <p>
    The writing is published under{' '}
    <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/" rel="license noopener">Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0</a>.
    In plain terms: take it, copy it, translate it, put it in your onboarding pack, sell a course that
    uses it. Two conditions. Say where it came from, and if you change it, publish your version under the
    same licence so the next person has the same freedom you did.
  </p>
  <p>
    The code that turns the notes into this website is separate, and is published under the MIT licence.
  </p>

  <h2>How It Is Built</h2>
  <p>
    The source of truth is a folder of plain text notes in{' '}
    <a href="https://obsidian.md/" rel="noopener">Obsidian</a>, one file per term, cross-linked the way
    Obsidian links things. A build script copies those notes across, works out what every link points at,
    and refuses to publish if a link points at a term that does not exist, if a term has no way in, or if
    two terms would end up at the same web address.
  </p>
  <p>
    The site itself is <a href="https://astro.build/" rel="noopener">Astro</a>, which turns the notes into
    ordinary HTML pages ahead of time, and it is hosted on{' '}
    <a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/static-assets/" rel="noopener">Cloudflare Workers</a>.
    There is no database and no content management system. The graph layout is calculated once during the
    build, so your browser only has to draw it, never work it out.
  </p>
  <p>
    Everything except the suggestion form is a static file, which is the whole security model: there is
    almost nothing running to attack.
  </p>

  <h2>Glossaries</h2>
  <ul class="sections">
    <li><a href="/ai/">Artificial intelligence</a>: {terms.length} terms in {groups.length} sections. Live.</li>
    <li>More to come. If you have a field that badly needs one, <a href="/suggest/glossary/">propose it</a>.</li>
  </ul>
</Base>
